  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nA few patches of low clouds are developing over the coastal waters\nthis morning, otherwise skies are clear. One last warm and dry day\nis in store before the weather takes a turn for the next week.\nHighs today will be anywhere from about 5-10 degrees above normal:\nupper 70s to mid 80s for the coastal areas to the inland valleys,\nupper 50s to upper 60s in the mountains, low to mid 70s in the\nhigh desert, and low to mid 80s in the low desert. \n\nCloud cover begins to increase later today as an upper level\ntrough approaches the West Coast. A closed low forms in the\nsouthern stream of the jet, moving over the California Bight and\nbecoming an open wave again as the vort max passes through\nnorthern Baja Friday morning. Given the trajectory of this low,\nthe highest rainfall amounts are expected near the coast late\ntonight into Friday morning, with showers becoming isolated and\nlighter during the afternoon as the trough moves out but we remain\nunder weakly cyclonic flow. For this first round of precipitation,\nchances of at least 0.25\" range from 45-65% along the coast,\n25-55% in the valleys and mountains, and less than 5% in the\ndeserts. Snow levels will be anywhere from 8000-8700 ft on Friday.\n\nAnother weak short wave trough moves through Friday night into \nSaturday morning, providing a round of more widespread light to\nmoderate showers. Chances of at least 0.25\" from 4 PM Fri-4 PM Sat\nrange from 65-85% for the coast to the mountains, 5-10% in the \nhigh desert, and less than 5% in the low deserts. Chances of 0.50\"\nor more range from around 45-70% for the coasts to the\nmountains and less than 5% in the deserts. The snow level remains\nupwards of 8500 ft with less than an inch expected above that\nelevation. Gusty west winds will also develop across the\nmountains and deserts Friday night into Saturday morning as the\ntrough moves through. Gusts 30-40 mph are expected across the\nridges, desert mountain slopes, high desert, and below the passes\nwith isolated gusts 45-55 mph on the wind-prone passes and desert\nslopes. \n\nTransient ridging moves over on Sunday for dry weather and minor\nwarming. This is short-lived as yet another upper level trough\namplifies along the West Coast and moves inland Monday afternoon\ninto Tuesday. Ensembles are in generally good agreement with the\ntiming of this trough, though there is some spread in the depth of\nthe trough, resulting in differences in rainfall amounts and snow\nlevels. In general this looks to be another light to moderate \nevent providing widespread beneficial rainfall with a 50-70%\nchance of at least 0.25\" for the coast to the mountains and a\n35-60% of at least 0.50\" for the coast to the mountains. NBM snow\nlevels fall to around 7000-7500 ft Tuesday morning with generally\na inch or less at that elevation, but up to 2-4 inches on the\nhighest peaks of the San Bernardino and San Jacinto Mountains. \nGusty onshore winds are anticipated again during this period. Weak\ncyclonic flow may maintain light precipitation behind this trough\non Wednesday, followed by yet another long wave trough moving \nacross the state sometime Thu-Fri, though ensemble solutions \nreally begin to diverge on the timing and amplitude of that \ntrough. Otherwise high temperatures will remain roughly 5-10\ndegrees below normal next week, a stark change from our stretch of\nwell above normal temperatures this past week.",
